            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"DIVERGENT\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The consensus understands 'the truth' primarily as the person of Jesus Christ and the New Testament gospel message or God's revealed word, while acknowledging secondary applications such as God's covenant faithfulness, moral integrity, and God's law.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The book defines 'the truth' strictly as God's law and the standard of righteousness, explicitly differentiating its view from the common understanding of Christ and the gospel.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Jesus Christ, the gospel, and God's revealed word.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"God's law.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book excludes Christ and the gospel from the definition of 'the truth,' restricting the term entirely to God's law.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The consensus views Christ and the New Testament gospel as the ultimate embodiment of 'the truth,' whereas the book rejects this primary identification (as evidenced by its 'Commonly taught' note) and replaces it with the law.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The consensus recognizes 'God's law' as a valid but secondary aspect of 'truth' (citing Psalm 119:142). However, the consensus overwhelmingly centers the primary meaning on Christ (John 14:6) and the gospel. The book elevates the law to the exclusive definition of 'the truth' while explicitly dismissing the primary Christological and gospel-centered consensus, making the core identification divergent even though its extension is a subset of the consensus.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The New Testament repeatedly and explicitly identifies Jesus Himself as 'the truth' (John 14:6) and the gospel as the 'word of truth' (Eph 1:13, Col 1:5). Limiting 'truth' exclusively to the law ignores the overwhelming Christological usage in the Johannine and Pauline writings.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
